# Kiran — environment configuration
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# .env is gitignored and must never be committed.
#
# Revised 2026-08-01 after the five-reviewer design review. Settings the review
# DISPROVED are marked ⚠ with what replaced them; see
# docs/REVIEW-2026-08-01.md. Spec references are to
# specs/autonomous_company_agent_spec.md.


# =============================================================================
# STAGE 1 — everything the shipped CLI actually reads
# =============================================================================
# `kiran quiet`, `kiran owed` and `kiran check` need only this block. No model,
# no database, no credentials. Every value has a working default, so an empty
# .env still runs.

# The Kuzu server from ~/projects/Gmail (scripts/serve.py). Kiran does not
# start it and never writes to it — KuzuClient refuses mutating statements.
#   cd ~/projects/Gmail && python scripts/serve.py --port 7001
COS_KUZU_URL=http://127.0.0.1:7001/kuzudb/graph

# Read-only. Kiran reads exactly two files: Task_management/Pipeline.md and
# Task_management/Prospects.md.
COS_VAULT_ROOT=/Users/YOU/vault

# Your own addresses, comma-separated. Mail from these is "outbound", mail to
# them is "inbound". weidong@kineviz.com is the only sender of Sent-labelled
# mail in the corpus (47,000 messages), so it is the default.
COS_PRINCIPAL_ADDRESSES=weidong@kineviz.com

# A named deal with no human contact for this many days is reported as quiet.
COS_QUIET_DAYS=30

# How far back `kiran owed` looks for unanswered mail.
COS_OWED_WINDOW_DAYS=90


# =============================================================================
# STAGE 3+ — PostgreSQL. NOT USED YET.
# =============================================================================
# Nothing below this line is read by any shipped code. Kept because the values
# were measured and the reasoning is worth not re-deriving.
#
# Custom image: postgres:19beta2 + pgvector 0.8.6 (verified to build).
# Container needs --shm-size=2g, or pgvector's parallel HNSW build fails
# silently and falls back to a path measured at 93 tuples/s (~3 h per 1M chunks).
#
# ⚠ Use PLAIN relational entity/relationship tables with recursive CTEs.
#   Do NOT use PG19 SQL/PGQ (CREATE PROPERTY GRAPH / GRAPH_TABLE):
#     - quantified paths (-[e]->{1,3}) are unsupported, so "who introduced us to
#       X" and entity-timeline traversal cannot be expressed at all
#     - it compiles to the same plan as a hand-written join (identical buffer
#       counts at 2 and 3 hops) at a 3.3x planning premium
#     - a shared label across entity tables OOM-kills the cluster at PLANNING
#       time; statement_timeout does not contain it
#     - mapping a generic relationship table returns 8 rows where 4 are correct,
#       with no error
#   Measured: recursive CTE 2.2 ms vs 73.7 ms for the PGQ workaround.
#
# 19beta2 will NOT pg_upgrade to the September GA release. The database is
# derived and disposable — but see the output cache below, without which
# "disposable" is fiction.

# COS_PG_HOST=127.0.0.1
# COS_PG_PORT=5434                # 5432 and 5433 are taken by hkjc-db, pg19beta
# COS_PG_DATABASE=kiran
# COS_PG_USER=kiran
# COS_PG_PASSWORD=REPLACE_ME

# ⚠ REVERSED: was /Volumes/FAST/kiran/pgdata.
#   The premise ("internal disk has ~63 GB free") was stale — df reports 154 GiB
#   — and the database projects to ~4 GB. More importantly, maildir and
#   maildir-live are SYMLINKS into /Volumes/FAST/Gmail, so putting PGDATA there
#   means one unplugged USB cable destroys the derived database AND the source
#   it is supposedly rebuildable from. Postgres on USB also risks fsync-ordering
#   corruption on surprise disconnect.
# COS_PG_DATA_DIR=/Users/YOU/.cos/pgdata

# ⚠ halfvec, not vector: 1,538 B/row instead of 3,076. Halves TOAST and HNSW
#   build I/O at negligible recall loss at 768 dims. Changing dims or model
#   requires a full re-embed.
# COS_EMBED_MODEL=nomic-embed-text
# COS_EMBED_DIMS=768
# COS_EMBED_VECTOR_TYPE=halfvec
# COS_EMBED_BATCH_SIZE=64         # measured optimum; embeddings do not
#                                   # parallelize (35-38 chunks/s at any worker count)

# Content-addressed model-output cache, keyed on
# sha256(normalized_body || model_id || prompt_version || params).
# 120 MB turns a 38-hour rebuild into ~2 hours. This is what makes spec §4.5
# true rather than aspirational, including across the beta2 -> GA migration.
# COS_CACHE_DIR=/Users/YOU/.cos/cache


# =============================================================================
# STAGE 4+ — local models. NOT USED YET.
# =============================================================================
# Measured on M2 Max / 32 GB. Untiered with thinking on, the 12-year backfill is
# 1,204 hours. Tiered with these settings it is 38.4 hours.

# OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434

# ⚠ MANDATORY. Defaults to 2, which makes any worker count above 2 inert —
#   extra workers only queue, inflating latency 3.5x for zero throughput.
# OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL=6

# ⚠ MANDATORY on 32 GB. qwen3:14b is 10.7 GB resident; loading it beside
#   qwen3:8b alongside Docker and Postgres thrashes swap.
# OLLAMA_MAX_LOADED_MODELS=1

# ⚠ MANDATORY. qwen3 emits ~2,400 chars of <think> per call by default: a
#   2.6-3.3x tax (641 output tokens vs 146) for zero classification benefit.
# COS_LOCAL_MODEL_THINK=false

# Tier by OUTPUT BUDGET first, model size second: gemma3:4b costs 0.109 s/msg
# at a 4-token verdict vs 1.58 s/msg at 190 tokens — 22x, same model.
# COS_LOCAL_MODEL_TRIAGE=gemma3:4b      # T1, 4-token verdict, 100% of corpus
# COS_LOCAL_MODEL=qwen3:8b              # T2, full JSON, ~33% of corpus
# COS_LOCAL_MODEL_DEEP=qwen3:14b        # T3, escalations only, ~3% of corpus

# -64% tokens, -57% chunks, and it removes the quoted text that caused 2 of 7
# measured extraction errors. A '>'-only stripper misses the 11.4% of mail using
# Outlook block quoting — which is most of the top counterparties.
# COS_QUOTE_STRIP=true


# =============================================================================
# STAGE 4+ — external model gateway. NOT USED YET.
# =============================================================================
# Reserved for thread-level synthesis (~$2 for the top 5,000 threads), NOT for
# per-message extraction. A bulk backfill through the gateway would ship 137,600
# private emails to a third party to save 28 hours, contradicts spec §6.7 (which
# assigns classification and basic extraction to local models), and trips the
# daily budget cap on day one.

# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-v1-REPLACE_ME
# COS_MODEL_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1

# Pinned dated snapshot, not a floating alias — an alias silently changes
# weights and breaks reproducibility. 1M ctx, $0.09/M in, $0.18/M out.
# COS_MODEL=deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash-0731

# ⚠ REMOVED: COS_MODEL_ZDR_ONLY.
#   The string "zdr" appears nowhere in the Hermes tree, and OpenRouter's public
#   endpoints API returns empty data_policy objects — so ZDR is a CONTRACTUAL
#   ASSERTION, not a technical control, and cannot be verified client-side. The
#   enforceable subset is exactly these three lines, plus recording the resolved
#   provider on every call and alerting on change.
# COS_MODEL_DATA_COLLECTION=deny
# COS_MODEL_ALLOW_FALLBACKS=false
# COS_MODEL_PROVIDER_PIN=

# ⚠ REDUCED from 200000. Spec §4.3 asks for "the current task, retrieved
#   evidence snippets, relevant entities" — 200k tokens is a quarter-mailbox,
#   i.e. the thing the cap exists to prevent.
# COS_MODEL_MAX_CONTEXT_TOKENS=32000
# COS_MODEL_DAILY_BUDGET_USD=10.00


# =============================================================================
# WRITE POLICY — applies from Stage 4 onward
# =============================================================================
# ⚠ NARROWED: 10_wiki and 05_workspace/Task_management were removed.
#   WORKSPACE_SPEC §5.6 grants write access to a HUMAN-DIRECTED Claude session,
#   not to an autonomous agent whose context is 156,000 attacker-authored
#   emails. Those are not the same actor. Separately, task files being writable
#   let an agent change a task owner — spec §8.1 "approval required" — via a
#   markdown write that the write-root model classifies as auto-approved.
#
#   Also: 10_wiki's frontmatter is already corrupted by a resolver bug
#   (Gmail/index/add_metadata.py strips the parenthetical disambiguator, then
#   substring-matches on from_name). Two different Claudia Brenners share one
#   address list; one Google Drive robot address is claimed as identity by 35
#   People pages. Seeding entity resolution from it and writing back would merge
#   35 humans.
# COS_WRITE_ROOTS=/Users/YOU/vault/90_agent

# Everything ingests most-restrictively (spec §15.9). Uniform today, so it
# partitions nothing until there is a second user.
# COS_DEFAULT_SCOPE=ceo_private

# Never log raw prompt or completion bodies (spec §9.5). Audit rows keep an
# inputs_hash and source_ids instead. Turning this on writes company email into
# plaintext logs — debugging only.
# COS_LOG_PROMPT_BODIES=false


# =============================================================================
# STAGE 5 — Hermes runtime. DEFERRED, possibly indefinitely.
# =============================================================================
# A pull-based CLI has none of the ten attack chains the security review found.
# If Hermes is ever adopted, these are mandatory, not optional:
#
#   HERMES_WRITE_SAFE_ROOT=<agent dir>   ← the ACTUAL control. COS_WRITE_ROOTS
#       constrains only Kiran's own MCP tools; Hermes' native write_file/patch
#       ignore it entirely. Verify it is set at startup; refuse to run if not.
#
#   agent.disabled_toolsets must be the authority, NOT an allowlist — it is
#       subtracted last and unconditionally, and is the only thing that survives
#       a per-cron-job enabled_toolsets override. Remove at minimum: terminal,
#       code_execution, browser, cronjob, skills, memory, web.
#
#   Removing terminal/browser/code_execution is NOT sufficient on its own:
#       write_file -> ~/.hermes/scripts/x.sh -> cronjob(script=, no_agent=true)
#       reaches arbitrary bash without touching any restricted tool.
#
#   approvals.mode: manual  (NOT "smart" — the default LLM guardian is itself
#       injectable). approvals.cron_mode: deny.
#
#   Never invoke `hermes -z`: oneshot.py:221 force-sets HERMES_YOLO_MODE=1
#       before tools.approval is imported, bypassing every approval check.
#
#   Approval state must live where the agent cannot write, and be bound to
#       sha256(proposal_body) so an approved proposal cannot be rewritten
#       before execution.
#
#   Scheduling belongs in launchd invoking the Kiran CLI, so the agent has no
#       scheduling primitive at all.
